{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1789691444666456949,"compiled_state":{"alias":"cerise-passive-milksnake","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":4,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"bb6d209b937fbc67e167b52ee3ad2ba44b369d8f307b61ad57f0759151f62d09","event_uuid":"3db6877b-43c9-4b10-bb4d-008222e3a2b1","position":{"commit_sha":"fedc760842a0d0a5c6e0b39fcbb7694cab51c21e","position":"1789672152886033695-3db6877b-43c9-4b10-bb4d-008222e3a2b1"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"4d00e18e392e2fe68119d21a9ae629f87926717c612b800dd17ee6c02943756b","event_uuid":"e1dd7e4c-54f8-42cb-8759-206e9006a127","position":{"commit_sha":"2b269075ce3db080a219d71ad252a0e1075ac094","position":"1789679623137712394-e1dd7e4c-54f8-42cb-8759-206e9006a127"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"5abf4aefb572af82fe26041700421e4a28ff0a4fffb6189c252c66b61568521d","event_uuid":"903292e4-dc04-4d35-98d6-57e41b6717d3","position":{"commit_sha":"ec47e1ce6d49c6ae0d64a03482e90dd177ad9f3c","position":"1789680807530493213-903292e4-dc04-4d35-98d6-57e41b6717d3"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"dfcb4e380195d46c2a9090434dd61c0461ea84a0410035cc6c026e8351e18acb","event_uuid":"ee1bd086-8c22-49a1-b92e-cbe00c9e5106","position":{"commit_sha":"b4226703a00a029abbee0c3281230ba3969d4e69","position":"1789681935805650381-ee1bd086-8c22-49a1-b92e-cbe00c9e5106"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## Additional evidence: the metered value moves without corresponding activity\n\nObserved while the session was deliberately running no billable LLM operations.\n\nThe Lambda's `metered` value held at exactly `352.1030` across roughly two hours of five-minute polls, then rose across two consecutive polls:\n\n```\n20:36Z  metered=352.1030  ce=0.0000\n20:41Z  metered=352.1030  ce=0.0000\n21:06Z  metered=357.7322  ce=0.0000\n21:11Z  metered=361.0880  ce=0.0000\n```\n\nDuring that window there was no plausible source of new Bedrock invocation:\n\n- No new Gerrit change or patchset since change 2977 merged at 19:10Z, so the review bot had nothing to review.\n- No gate operations were run — no plan review, no completion verification.\n- The only workflows that executed were `Mirror Guard`, `Reconcile Bridge`, and `Reconciler Heartbeat Canary`, none of which invoke Bedrock.\n\nThe most likely reading is that `metered` is derived from CloudWatch invocation metrics that arrive in batches well after the fact, so the rise is delayed accounting for usage already incurred earlier in the day rather than new usage. That is a hypothesis, not a conclusion — it was not traced to the metric publication path.\n\nEither way it compounds the defect this ticket already describes. The value that drives both the warning and the deny action is not only computed from a fallback rate for the primary model family, it also does not correspond in time to the activity that produced it. A consumer cannot distinguish \"spend is happening now\" from \"accounting for earlier spend is arriving now\", and those call for opposite responses: the first warrants stopping work, the second does not.\n\nThis matters for the pause guidance in `gleesome-hominine-merganser`. An agent that pauses on a rising meter may be reacting to activity it already stopped, and would resume only on a day boundary despite the underlying spend having ceased hours earlier.\n\nWorth establishing as part of this ticket's investigation: the actual lag characteristics of the metric source, so that consumers can be told which question the number answers.\n","timestamp":1789679623137712394},{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## Correction: the meter movement was real spend, not delayed accounting\n\nThe prior comment's hypothesis is wrong and should not be relied on. It proposed that `metered` rising from `352.1030` to `361.0880` with no local activity indicated batched, late-arriving metric accounting for earlier usage.\n\nThe actual cause is concurrent authorized work by another session against the same account. The rise tracked live spend correctly.\n\n**What this means for the ticket.** The metering lag concern raised in that comment is unevidenced — I inferred a measurement defect from an absence I had no authority to claim, having checked only this session's activity and this repository's Gerrit and Actions state. Those sources cannot see another session, so \"no plausible source of new invocation\" was a statement about my visibility, not about the account.\n\nThe evidence gathered in that comment does not support a timing defect and that line of investigation should not be pursued on its basis. Anyone picking this ticket up should treat the acceptance criteria as scoped to what remains genuinely established: the rate card carries no entry for the Anthropic Claude family, every invocation logs a fail-closed fallback for it, and the published figure carries no marker distinguishing a measured value from a partly-synthetic one. Those were verified directly from the rate card contents and the Lambda logs, and are unaffected by this correction.\n\n**A real finding does survive, and it is about the consumer rather than the meter.** The account-wide figure is shared across concurrent sessions. A session reading it cannot attribute any portion of it to its own work, so it cannot distinguish its own consumption from a peer's. For a guard whose response is \"stop working\", that distinction matters: a session may pause on movement it did not cause and cannot influence, while the work actually consuming the budget continues.\n\nThat is a property of a shared counter rather than a defect in the rate card, so it is noted here as context rather than folded into this ticket's criteria.\n","timestamp":1789680807530493213},{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Read-only investigation complete. Root cause confirmed, with ground truth established against a settled Cost Explorer day. Numbers are deliberately omitted here — the tracker is public; they were reported to the operator directly.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe Lambda does not build its rate card from the AWS Price List API. It builds it from Cost Explorer, filtered to exactly one service:\n\n```python\nce.get_cost_and_usage(\n    TimePeriod={\"Start\": start.isoformat(), \"End\": end.isoformat()},\n    Granularity=\"MONTHLY\",\n    Metrics=[\"UnblendedCost\", \"UsageQuantity\"],\n    Filter={\"Dimensions\": {\"Key\": \"SERVICE\", \"Values\": [\"Amazon Bedrock\"]}},\n    GroupBy=[{\"Type\": \"DIMENSION\", \"Key\": \"USAGE_TYPE\"}],\n)\n```\n\nCurrent Claude 4.x usage is **not billed under that service**. Cost Explorer exposes it as separate services — `Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Amazon Bedrock Edition)`, `Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Amazon Bedrock Edition)`, `Claude Opus 4.7 (Amazon Bedrock Edition)`, and siblings. The filter names the literal legacy service, so it collects rates for DeepSeek, Nova, Titan, Llama, Pixtral, gpt-oss, Mistral and minimax, and structurally cannot collect Anthropic.\n\nTwo alternative hypotheses were tested and eliminated. Pagination is not the cause — the response carried no `NextPageToken`. Staleness is not the cause — `rates_day` is current, `rate_count` is 33, and `anthropic_or_claude_rate_keys` is empty on a freshly refreshed card.\n\n## The fallback is not flat\n\n`_rate_for()` applies metric multipliers to `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`, and those multipliers mirror Claude's own input/output/cache ratios closely. The consequence is counterintuitive and worth stating: because the ratios are right and only the base is wrong, the error factor is **constant** across input, output, cache-read and cache-write. Workload mix does not dilute it. An input-heavy gate workload is mispriced by exactly the same factor as an output-heavy one.\n\nThe Sonnet 4.5 component is overpriced by a factor of roughly 22.7x. Overall the live daily figure runs about 5.3x high, the difference being the model families that `SEED_RATES` already covers correctly.\n\n## Ground truth\n\nFor settled day 2026-09-16, reconstructing the Lambda's CloudWatch-token algorithm and comparing against settled Cost Explorer for all Bedrock Edition services gives a metered figure about 1.32x the settled one. Nearly the entire overage is the Sonnet 4.5 fallback; the other Claude families were priced by `SEED_RATES` and matched settled CE for that day. So the defect is narrow and precisely located: it is the absence of a Sonnet 4.5 rate, not a broken pricing path in general.\n\n## `ce_spend` cannot reconcile it\n\n`_ce_spend_today()` carries the identical broken filter:\n\n```python\nFilter={\"Dimensions\": {\"Key\": \"SERVICE\", \"Values\": [\"Amazon Bedrock\"]}}\n```\n\nSo `ce_spend` never sees Claude spend even after Cost Explorer settles. The Lambda computes `spend = max(ce_spend, metered)` and never compares the reconstructed meter against settled all-Claude CE, so the discrepancy is never surfaced. The safety net that would have caught this has the same hole as the thing it was meant to check.\n\n## `tripped_day` is stale and misleading\n\nThe persisted `tripped_day` records 2026-09-13, but logs show that was **not** a spend event. At `2026-09-13T04:32:51Z` the Lambda logged all-zero metered and CE values with the threshold temporarily configured to zero, attached the deny policy on that basis, and released minutes later when the threshold returned to normal. Settled CE for that day was effectively nil. The flag is evidence of a zero-threshold config event, not of a spend cap firing.\n\n## Deployment state\n\nThere is no `bedrock-spend-cap` source or Terraform resource anywhere under `infra/`. A search found only unrelated Lambdas (`auth_host`, the SSO gate). The live function is deployed directly in AWS — `python3.12`, handler `handler.handler`, last modified `2026-09-14T16:53:52Z`. So \"deployed matches repo\" cannot be answered, because nothing in the repo manages it. That absence is itself a finding and may deserve its own ticket.\n\n## Fix direction\n\nMove the rate source to the AWS Pricing API, or broaden CE discovery to all Bedrock Edition foundation-model services. **Do not simply add `Claude*` to the service filter** — that would appear to work and then misprice by a further large factor, because Claude Marketplace usage types differ structurally:\n\n- usage types are shaped `USE1-MP:USE1_InputTokenCount-Units`, while the current suffix matcher expects forms like `input-tokens`;\n- Claude is priced per 1M tokens, so the derived `cost / UsageQuantity` would be per-1M while the rest of the card is per-1K.\n\nSo a correct fix needs service-to-model mapping, usage-type normalization for Marketplace `*-Units` forms, explicit unit conversion, and — importantly — a `published` versus `estimated` marker whenever any fallback or seed participates in the figure, so an enforcement action can never again be taken on a synthetic number without that being visible.\n\nA narrower interim measure exists: `SEED_RATES` already prices the other Claude families correctly, so seeding Sonnet 4.5 would remove the dominant error without restructuring the discovery path.\n\nNo AWS state was modified. No LLM gate operation was run.","timestamp":1789681935805650381}],"created_at":1789672152886033695,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[],"description":"## Problem\n\nThe `bedrock-spend-cap` Lambda prices Bedrock usage from a rate card held in SSM `/bedrock-spend-cap/state` under `rates`. That card contains no entry for any Anthropic Claude model. Every invocation logs a fail-closed fallback for the model family that rebar's own LLM operations actually run on:\n\n```\n[WARNING] no rate for model=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 metric=InputTokenCount; using fail-closed fallback\n[WARNING] no rate for model=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 metric=CacheWriteInputTokenCount; using fail-closed fallback\n[WARNING] no rate for model=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 metric=OutputTokenCount; using fail-closed fallback\n[WARNING] no rate for model=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 metric=CacheReadInputTokenCount; using fail-closed fallback\n```\n\nThese four lines repeat on every scheduled run. The rate card does carry entries for DeepSeek, Llama 3.3, Nova (Lite/Micro/Pro/2.0 Lite), Pixtral, Titan embeddings, gpt-oss, Mistral Large 3, and MiniMax — that is, for models the project does not primarily use, while the primary family is unpriced.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe Lambda drives two control actions. The lower threshold emits a warning that project guidance now uses to pause all agent work until the next day. The upper threshold attaches a deny policy to every target role, which takes down the review bot and every rebar gate at once. Both actions are therefore triggered by a number that, for the dominant workload, is a synthetic fallback rather than a measured rate.\n\nThe direction of the error is unknown and matters in both directions:\n\n- If the fallback over-prices, the project pauses work it did not need to pause and eventually denies Bedrock access while genuinely under budget. The cost is lost delivery time and a self-inflicted outage.\n- If the fallback under-prices, real overspend runs past both thresholds without either firing. The cost is an unbounded bill and a control that reports healthy while failing.\n\nNeither can currently be ruled out, because the fallback rate's relationship to actual Claude pricing is not established anywhere in the parameter or the logs.\n\nThis is the same class of defect as `enviable-beloved-buffalo` (a metric that cannot change state carries no information) and `catty-giddied-hedgehog` (an alarm latched for days without anyone owning it). A control whose input is not trustworthy is not a control, whichever way it errs.\n\n## Investigation notes\n\n`rates_day` is `2026-09-17`, so the card is being refreshed — the omission is not staleness. This points at the rate-loading path filtering out or failing to resolve the Anthropic entries, rather than at an out-of-date snapshot. That is a hypothesis and needs confirming against the loader before any fix.\n\nNote also that the fallback is silent in effect: it emits a `WARNING` to the Lambda log, but the resulting spend figure is published to SSM with no marker distinguishing a measured value from a partly-synthetic one. A consumer reading the parameter cannot tell that the number is estimated.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The root cause is confirmed by observation: identify why the rate loader yields no Anthropic entries while populating other providers, rather than assuming staleness or an upstream omission.\n- [ ] The fallback rate's actual relationship to real Claude pricing is established and recorded, so the historical direction and rough magnitude of the error is known rather than guessed.\n- [ ] A failing test reproduces the condition before any fix: a rate card lacking the primary model family must be detectable rather than silently tolerated.\n- [ ] Anthropic Claude models used by rebar's LLM operations are priced from real rates.\n- [ ] An unpriced model in the workload is surfaced as an actionable signal, not only as a log line — a spend figure computed with any fallback component is marked as estimated where it is published, so consumers can tell a measured value from a synthetic one.\n- [ ] Threshold behaviour is re-validated after the fix, so the warning and deny actions are known to fire against real spend.\n","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"3083-ada3-3a0f-4051","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Bedrock spend cap cannot price the primary model family and falls back silently, so both thresholds fire on a synthetic number","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["3db6877b-43c9-4b10-bb4d-008222e3a2b1","e1dd7e4c-54f8-42cb-8759-206e9006a127","903292e4-dc04-4d35-98d6-57e41b6717d3","ee1bd086-8c22-49a1-b92e-cbe00c9e5106"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1789691444666456949,"uuid":"8012bbaf-a81e-48f0-8589-bf2c1069760e"}